  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued unsettled and humid this afternoon. \nWidespread showers and a few thunderstorms early, then scattered\nafternoon showers/storms possible. Mainly dry with skies \nclearing tonight.\n\nA warm front that lifted through the area last night and this \nmorning is currently situated across the northern Mid-Atlantic. Most \nof the shower activity has shifted to SE VA and NE NC, with \nscattered showers and a few isolated lightning strikes. There has \nbeen enough steering flow to push these showers along rather than \nletting them linger over one area for too long, so there have been \nno localized flooding issues so far today. Satellite imagery shows \nsome clearing across the SE portion of the forecast area, which has \nhelped with the aforementioned shower development. The remained of \nthe forecast area aside from the far NW piedmont is still entrenched \nin thick cloud cover that has kept temperatures capped around 80 \ndegrees. However, these clouds are finally starting to scatter out, \nwhich will allow temperatures to reach the upper 80s for most of the \narea. With dew points in the lower to mid 70s and generally light \nsurface winds, it feels quite muggy outside (especially for almost \nmid-September). Scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms will \ncontinue to develop across the SE portion of the area, with some hi-\nres guidance suggesting a few isolated showers could develop across \nthe piedmont later this afternoon and evening. A cold front \ncurrently draped well to our northwest will gradually slide \nsoutheastward tonight and tomorrow. Ahead of this front, SW-W flow \nwill prevail and dew points and temperatures will remain elevated \nfor one last night. Low temperatures tonight are forecast to be in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. Precipitation and cloud cover will start \nto clear out overnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures and \nsignificantly lower humidity Monday-Wednesday. Temperatures warm \nback above normal late in the week an upper ridge builds back into \nthe region. Next chance for rain Fri-Sat.\n\nThe upper level flow becomes NW Monday as a strong upper level \ntrough dives SE from the Great Lakes and then lifts offshore on \nMonday night. Meanwhile, an upper level ridge is forecast to slowly \nexpand NE from the lower MS Valley. A stronger cold front will drop \nacross the forecast on Monday and bring significantly lower dew \npoints that are more typical of September. With a strong upper low \ndipping down across the area, increasing cloud cover is expected for \na few hours on Monday with slight chance PoPs lingering through the \nafternoon across the southern areas before the much drier air surges \nin from the NNW during the afternoon/evening. \n\nAs high pressure builds in behind the front Monday night, a pleasant \nnight is expected Monday night/Tue AM with lows mostly in the mid-\nupper 50s to lower 60s (low-mid 50s are likely in many locations to \nthe NW of Richmond). The upper level ridge becomes nearly stationary \nacross the TN Valley Tue-Wed as a weak upper level trough develops \noff the SE Coast. This pattern will allow strong surface high \npressure (~1030 mb), to set up from off the New England coast SW \ninto the Carolina coastal plain through midweek. Highs will stay \nmostly in the low-mid 80s through Wed with lows in the 50s to lower \n60s (locally warmer at the immediate coast). The models now mostly \nhave the upper tough to our S/SE potentially amplifying late in the \nweek which would retrograde the ridge back towards the lower MS \nValley. Overall, a decent amount of uncertainty as to how this \nevolves, but the forecast will continue to show temperatures \nclimbing back to above normal for mid-September, with some low-end \nrain chances Fri-Sat.",
